{{<html}}
{{$main}}

{{& page.contents.html}}

<section class="home-hero">
    <div class="home-hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Server-side Swift, structured for real applications</p>
        <h1>Build modular Swift backends without rebuilding the foundation.</h1>
        <p class="lead">Feather Framework gives you focused backend components for database access, storage, mail, HTTP clients, and OpenAPI integration. FATHOMS shows how to organize those pieces into clear modules, layers, use cases, and adapters.</p>
        <div class="home-actions">
            <a class="cta" href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
            <a class="secondary-link" href="/docs/getting-started/introduction/">What is Feather?</a>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="home-hero-panel" aria-label="Feather application structure">
        <div class="panel-header">
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-card accent-blue">
            <strong>HTTP server</strong>
            <span>Vapor or Hummingbird</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-card accent-purple">
            <strong>Application</strong>
            <span>Use cases and ports</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-card accent-green">
            <strong>Infrastructure</strong>
            <span>Database, storage, mail, APIs</span>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-card accent-slate">
            <strong>Domain</strong>
            <span>Business rules and models</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why Feather</p>
        <h2>A practical foundation for Swift backends.</h2>
        <p>Most backend applications need the same building blocks. Feather packages those common concerns behind small Swift APIs, so your application can stay organized as it grows.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="home-card">
            <h3>Reusable components</h3>
            <p>Use focused packages for database access, mail, storage, HTTP clients, and OpenAPI without tying your app to one large framework.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="home-card">
            <h3>Clear application structure</h3>
            <p>FATHOMS gives your code predictable places for modules, layers, use cases, interfaces, and infrastructure adapters.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="home-card">
            <h3>Works with your HTTP server</h3>
            <p>Feather works with Hummingbird and Vapor. Your HTTP framework handles requests while Feather helps structure the application around it.</p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Components</p>
        <h2>Use the parts your backend needs.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="component-grid">
        <a class="component-card" href="/docs/database/">
            <span>Database</span>
            <p>A common database API with drivers for SQLite, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="component-card" href="/docs/getting-started/components/">
            <span>Mail</span>
            <p>Send email through a clean interface without spreading provider details through your app.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="component-card" href="/docs/getting-started/components/">
            <span>Storage</span>
            <p>Organize file storage behind a stable abstraction for local or remote providers.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="component-card" href="/docs/getting-started/components/">
            <span>HTTP</span>
            <p>Call external services through reusable client components and consistent boundaries.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="component-card" href="/docs/getting-started/components/">
            <span>OpenAPI</span>
            <p>Keep API contracts close to your application and generate reliable integration points.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="component-card" href="/docs/getting-started/architecture/">
            <span>FATHOMS</span>
            <p>Structure modules, layers, use cases, repositories, and adapters with a clear pattern.</p>
        </a>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-band">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Server layer</p>
        <h2>Bring Vapor or Hummingbird.</h2>
        <p>Feather is not trying to replace your HTTP server. Vapor and Hummingbird can handle routing, middleware, request parsing, and responses. Feather sits at the application structure level and helps organize the deeper backend concerns.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="secondary-link" href="/docs/getting-started/http-servers/">Compare the server options</a>
</section>

<section class="home-section split-section">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
        <h2>FATHOMS keeps the project understandable.</h2>
        <p>FATHOMS is the application architecture used with Feather. It separates the domain, application use cases, infrastructure adapters, modules, and server layer so teams can change one part without tangling the rest.</p>
        <a class="secondary-link" href="/docs/getting-started/architecture/">Learn the architecture</a>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-list">
        <div><strong>Features</strong><span>Reusable business areas</span></div>
        <div><strong>Application</strong><span>Use cases and interfaces</span></div>
        <div><strong>Infrastructure</strong><span>Database tables and adapters</span></div>
        <div><strong>Server</strong><span>HTTP delivery through Vapor or Hummingbird</span></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section latest-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Latest</p>
        <h2>News and updates.</h2>
    </div>

    {{#empty(context.posts)}}
    <p>There are no updates yet.</p>
    {{/empty(context.posts)}}
    {{^empty(context.posts)}}
    <div class="grid grid-321">
    {{#context.posts}}
        {{> partials.blog.post}}
    {{/context.posts}}
    </div>
    {{/empty(context.posts)}}

    <div class="centered">
        <a href="/articles/page/1/" class="secondary-link">Browse all articles</a>
    </div>
</section>

{{/main}}
{{/html}}
